   "title": "N2.45tn Liquidity Inflow Expected in Banking System as DMO Plans N800bn T-bills",
   "url": "https://www.thisdaylive.com/2026/11/11/n2-45tn-liquidity-inflow-expected-in-banking-system-as-dmo-plans-n800bn-t-bills/",
   "domain": "thisdaylive.com",
   "source": "ThisDay（尼日利亚）",
   "published": "Tue, 10 Nov 2026 23:33:00 +0000",
   "_dt": 1794353580.0,
   "ageDays": 1.02,
   "summary": "Nigeria’s banking system is expected to receive N2.448 trillion in liquidity inflows this week, largely driven by maturing Open Market Operations (OMO) bills, even as the Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN) and the Debt Management Office (DMO) prepare fresh debt auctions to absorb excess cash from the financial system.",
   "summary_zh": "预计尼日利亚银行系统本周将获得 2.448 万亿奈拉流动性流入，主要由到期的公开市场操作（OMO）票据推动；与此同时，尼日利亚中央银行（CBN）和债务管理办公室（DMO）正准备举行新的债券拍卖，以吸收金融系统中的过剩现金。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Nigeria’s banking system is expected to receive N2.448 trillion in liquidity inflows this week, largely driven by maturing Open Market Operations (OMO) bills, even as the Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN) and the Debt Management Office (DMO) prepare fresh debt auctions to absorb excess cash from the financial system. This is according to the Weekly Market Report by the Financial Markets Dealers Association (FMDA), where it projected that OMO maturities would account for N2.298 trillion of the expected inflows, while Treasury bills maturities would contribute N148.565 billion. The anticipated liquidity injection comes against the backdrop of sustained efforts by the CBN to manage excess naira liquidity, with the apex bank raising N5.667 trillion through OMO auctions conducted on November 3 and 6. The anticipated inflows are expected to test the CBN’s liquidity management strategy, particularly as the DMO prepares to raise fresh funds from the market. The N800 billion offer, against only N148.565 billion in Treasury bills maturities, also provides an avenue to withdraw liquidity from the financial system, alongside potential further OMO interventions. Meanwhile, the foreign exchange market recorded divergent movements, with the naira appreciating at the official window but weakening at the parallel market. The country’s external reserves also declined marginally by 0.16 per cent week-on-week to $55.133 billion, following their recent rise above the $55 billion mark, the highest level since December 2008. In the international oil market, average Brent crude fell by 13.45 per cent to $99.97 per barrel, retreating from an intraday high above $116 recorded the previous week. FMDA attributed the decline to easing Middle East supply concerns following signs of de-escalation involving Iran after the United States midterm elections. In the fixed-income market, average FGN bond yields rose by 20 basis points to 16.37 per cent, driven largely by the seven-year instrument, which increased by 104 basis points. Conversely, Treasury bills yields declined by 35 basis points to 17.67 per cent, reflecting a divergence in movements across both segments of the market. Founded on January 22, 1995, THISDAY is published by THISDAY NEWSPAPERS LTD., 35 Creek Road Apapa, Lagos, Nigeria with offices in 36 states of Nigeria , the Federal Capital Territory and around the world. It is Nigeria’s most authoritative news media available on all platforms for the political, business, professional and diplomatic elite and broader middle classes while serving as the meeting point of new ideas, culture and technology for the aspirationals and millennials. The newspaper is a public trust dedicated to the pursuit of truth and reason covering a range of issues from breaking news to politics, business, the markets, the arts, sports and community to the crossroads of people and society. You can email us at: hello@thisdaylive.com or visit our contact us page",
